# Secrets stay on the Cloudflare dashboard

Do not touch secrets. Not with Wrangler, MCP, the Cloudflare API, shell, SQL, or files. Not to inspect, not to fix, not after the user says the task needs one.

A secret is any API key, token, password, or connection string: `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `APIFY_TOKEN`, `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY`, worker secrets, Fly secrets, and the same names in `.dev.vars` or `.env*`.

## Never

- `wrangler secret list`, `put`, `delete`, or `bulk`
- Read, create, update, or delete secrets through MCP or the Cloudflare API, including secret names from a list call
- Open, grep, print, copy, or commit `.dev.vars`, `.env*`, or a secret value
- Ask the user to paste a secret into chat
- Put a secret value in `wrangler.toml`, source, tests, or docs

## When a secret has to change

Stop. Tell the user, and they will do it manually in the Cloudflare dashboard:

- which Worker and environment (staging or production)
- the secret name
- create, replace, or delete, and why

Then wait. Do not run the command for them.

Plain text `[vars]` in `wrangler.toml` (feature flags, model ids) are not secrets. Deploys stay `npx wrangler deploy --env <env> --keep-vars` and do not include a secret step. See `wrangler-deploy-safety.mdc`.
